WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.967
Database access should keep data separate
from query structure.

2
00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:12.906
Safe ORM patterns and parameterized
queries help prevent injection when they are

3
00:00:12.986 --> 00:00:14.377
used correctly.

4
00:00:14.457 --> 00:00:19.228
Raw SQL, dynamic filters, user-controlled
sort fields,

5
00:00:19.308 --> 00:00:24.080
and query construction helpers deserve
careful review.

6
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:31.118
Least-privilege database accounts also
matter because the web service should not

7
00:00:31.198 --> 00:00:37.261
connect with more database capability than
it needs to perform its role.

8
00:00:37.341 --> 00:00:42.609
Mass assignment and over-posting are
important API concerns.

9
00:00:42.689 --> 00:00:47.659
If request models are bound too broadly to
database models,

10
00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:53.206
clients may influence fields that should
be server-controlled,

11
00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:59.946
such as owner, tenant, role, status,
price, approval state, or audit metadata.

12
00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:06.885
Defensive code chooses which fields can
come from the client and which fields must

13
00:01:06.965 --> 00:01:13.625
come from authentication context,
authorization decisions, workflow state,

14
00:01:13.705 --> 00:01:15.693
or server-side policy.

15
00:01:15.773 --> 00:01:19.749
File uploads create a separate trust
boundary.

16
00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:25.992
A safe service considers file size,
expected content, extension policy,

17
00:01:26.072 --> 00:01:32.433
storage location, filename handling,
temporary cleanup, authorization,

18
00:01:32.513 --> 00:01:34.899
and downstream processing.

19
00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:40.645
Uploaded files may be parsed later,
scanned, transformed, stored,

20
00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:43.011
or passed to other systems.

21
00:01:43.091 --> 00:01:50.148
That means validation and access control
should continue after the initial request

22
00:01:50.228 --> 00:01:51.819
has been accepted.

23
00:01:51.899 --> 00:01:58.956
Background tasks and serialization need
security review because they may continue

24
00:01:59.036 --> 00:02:02.416
processing after the response returns.

25
00:02:02.496 --> 00:02:07.565
Delayed work may use data, permissions,
identities, files,

26
00:02:07.645 --> 00:02:11.820
or external actions captured during the
request.

27
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:18.659
JSON, YAML, XML, and CSV processing should
use safe parser modes, clear schemas,

28
00:02:18.739 --> 00:02:20.727
and reasonable limits.

29
00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:27.566
Unsafe deserialization at a high level is
the risk of turning untrusted data into

30
00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:32.020
behavior or objects the application did
not intend.
